faq:
  - q: "What is Airbnb's hiring process?"
    a: "Airbnb's process: (1) Application via Airbnb Careers or recruiter reach-out. (2) Recruiter phone screen — background, role fit, culture alignment. (3) Role-specific screen (coding for SWE, portfolio for design, case study for PM). (4) Virtual onsite loop — 4–5 rounds. (5) Hiring manager debrief and offer. Airbnb places significant emphasis on culture fit — the culture interview round is taken as seriously as technical skill. The process typically takes 5–8 weeks. Note: Airbnb went fully remote in 2022 ('Live and Work Anywhere' policy), which significantly expanded its hiring geography."
  - q: "What is Airbnb's culture and what does it look for in candidates?"
    a: "Airbnb's culture is built around six core values: Champion the Mission (belong anywhere), Be a Host (create belonging), Embrace the Adventure, Be a Cereal Entrepreneur (resourceful and scrappy), Simplify, and Be a Storyteller. The most heavily assessed in interviews: Champion the Mission (genuine belief in Airbnb's role in the world), Be a Host (putting the needs of guests, hosts, and colleagues first), and Embrace the Adventure (comfort with ambiguity, experimentation, and rapid change). Airbnb interviews candidates who genuinely use and love the product — experienced Airbnb travelers have a meaningful advantage."
  - q: "How important is cultural alignment at Airbnb?"
    a: "Extremely important — more so than at most tech companies. Airbnb has a dedicated culture interview round and interviewers across all rounds explicitly assess values alignment. The company was founded on a deeply personal mission (the founders slept on air mattresses in their SF apartment to survive — hence the name). Candidates who have a genuine, personal connection to the mission of belonging and travel convert at higher rates than those who frame it as a career move."
  - q: "How are Airbnb's software engineering interviews structured?"
    a: "Airbnb's SWE interviews include: coding rounds (LeetCode medium, with emphasis on strings, arrays, and graphs), system design at senior levels (Airbnb-specific systems: booking, search, pricing, fraud detection), and a culture/values round. Airbnb's coding environment has historically included a 'coding challenge' on HackerRank before the onsite. At senior levels (E5+), system design is weighted heavily. Airbnb design interviews are notable for being relatively collaborative — interviewers often guide candidates rather than withholding information."
  - q: "What makes Airbnb's design and PM interviews unique?"
    a: "Airbnb is one of the most design-centric tech companies in the world — the founders are designers, and design sits at the center of product decisions. PM and design interviews reflect this: candidates are expected to have genuine opinions about user experience, visual design, and the emotional experience of hosting or traveling. Generic product frameworks without design thinking fail at Airbnb. The question 'How would you improve the Airbnb host experience?' requires UX thinking, empathy, and data fluency together."
